1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
When we think of organized crime, well, the mental image is almost always linked to

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:08,839
blatant actions, right, gunfights, overt violence, in short, movie scenes.

3
00:00:08,839 --> 00:00:11,000
But there's a fundamental question to ask.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,880
Is the strength of these organizations truly and I emphasize truly based solely on physical intimidation?

5
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:16,879
physical?

6
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:21,679
Or is there a much quieter and incredibly even more pervasive method to gain and maintain

7
00:00:21,679 --> 00:00:22,679
power?

8
00:00:22,679 --> 00:00:26,280
So, in this analysis, we will explore a real economic mystery.

9
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:30,679
We will discover how criminal groups manage to distort the legal economy from within,

10
00:00:30,679 --> 00:00:34,480
literally taking control of institutions without even needing to draw a

11
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:35,480
weapon.

12
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:38,760
It's time to take a closer look at the invisible dynamics that govern public

13
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:39,760
finances.

14
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:41,920
On one hand, we have the common perception.

15
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:45,159
Crime measured through murders and violent offenses.

16
00:00:45,159 --> 00:00:49,960
However, economic research by Marco di Cataldo and Nicola Maestro Rocco presents us with

17
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:51,560
a very different reality.

18
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:57,200
Today, true power is consolidated through silent agreements, the so-called white-collar

19
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:58,200
crimes.

20
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,200
Violence is just the proverbial tip of the iceberg.

21
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:05,700
The truly devastating economic impact occurs when these organizations sit at

22
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:10,719
the tables where financial decisions are made, blending almost perfectly

23
00:01:10,719 --> 00:01:13,320
into the slow state bureaucracy.

24
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:18,260
Politics and organized crime, the core of this whole affair lies precisely

25
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:19,840
in this intersection.

26
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:24,439
It's not about threatening politicians from the outside, but about capturing them from within.

27
00:01:24,439 --> 00:01:29,180
The objective is to guide the hand of those who decide where public money goes.

28
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:33,740
We witness a total manipulation in which criminals effectively become

29
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:38,020
hidden, yet very powerful, partners of local administrators.

30
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:43,020
To understand exactly what we're talking about, we need a clear definition.

31
00:01:43,019 --> 00:01:48,780
We're talking about criminal infiltration when illicit groups capture local politicians

32
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:52,859
to manipulate public decisions entirely in their favor.

33
00:01:52,859 --> 00:01:58,140
The study we are examining analyzes this phenomenon over a critical timeframe that

34
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:01,420
spans from 1998 to 2016.

35
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:05,019
And pay attention, we're not talking about casual corruption.

36
00:02:05,019 --> 00:02:10,379
We're talking about a genuine, systematic strategy, meticulously planned, to bend

37
00:02:10,379 --> 00:02:14,379
the public machine to the economic interests of crime.

38
00:02:14,379 --> 00:02:19,799
Measuring mafia infiltration, here arises the great challenge for economists: how does one

39
00:02:19,799 --> 00:02:24,620
quantify a phenomenon that was specifically designed not to be seen?

40
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:31,500
The solution, honestly brilliant, was found in Law 164 of 1991.

41
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:36,139
This legislation allows for the dissolution of municipal councils precisely when evidence emerges

42
00:02:36,139 --> 00:02:39,699
of collusion between elected officials and the mafia.

43
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:45,580
Using the application of this law effectively provides a documented and unequivocal map

44
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:49,180
of where and when infiltration became a reality.

45
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:53,700
Looking at the geographical distribution of these dissolutions, we immediately realize

46
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:56,820
that infiltration is not an abstract theory by scholars.

47
00:02:56,820 --> 00:03:01,780
It is a tangible, measurable phenomenon, with a very heavy impact on specific areas of the

48
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:03,260
national territory.

49
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:07,940
The density of dissolutions that emerges from the data indicates, without mincing words,

50
00:03:07,939 --> 00:03:11,579
the areas where criminal presence has ceased to be an exception and has become

51
00:03:11,579 --> 00:03:12,579
systemic.

52
00:03:12,579 --> 00:03:16,579
Inevitably, the focus of the analysis narrows to Southern Italy.

53
00:03:16,579 --> 00:03:21,939
Calabria, Campania, and Sicily form the main sample of this study, because they represent

54
00:03:21,939 --> 00:03:26,740
the regions where the historical roots of criminal organizations are deepest.

55
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:31,060
For this very reason, they offer researchers the amount of data needed to fully understand

56
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:35,259
the economic consequences, the real ones, of these illicit agreements.

57
00:03:35,259 --> 00:03:40,539
The impact on public resources, after establishing where infiltration occurs

58
00:03:40,539 --> 00:03:45,620
and how to measure it, there's a huge analytical question to answer: how does this

59
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:51,139
political capture concretely alter public finances and citizens' money, when a

60
00:03:51,139 --> 00:03:54,139
municipality falls under the influence of crime?

61
00:03:54,139 --> 00:03:59,859
This is the real playing field, and it's here that spending decisions are literally

62
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:02,820
diverted to fuel the illegal economy.

63
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:06,180
But how does this mechanism work in practice?

64
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:08,300
It unfolds in three key steps.

65
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:10,460
First step: elections.

66
00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:15,040
Be careful, here they are not just seen as a democratic moment, but truly serve as a

67
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:19,740
recruitment process in which criminal groups select their ideal political

68
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:21,500
counterpart.

69
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:26,460
Second step, the agreement: a black-on-white pact is struck, very often even before

70
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:29,220
citizens go to vote.

71
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:32,660
Third and final step, the distortion of public funds.

72
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:37,940
Once in power, the municipality's priorities magically change; they no longer follow the needs

73
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:41,420
of citizens, but rather the strategic demands of organized crime.

74
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:47,420
There's a number that largely explains why this system manages to thrive undisturbed:

75
00:04:47,420 --> 00:04:49,420
71%.

76
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:54,140
This is the impressive turnover rate of local administrators in these regions.

77
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:57,020
In practice, they leave politics within five years.

78
00:04:57,019 --> 00:05:02,060
One might think that this constant instability is a problem, but no, it's a huge

79
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:03,060
opportunity.

80
00:05:03,060 --> 00:05:07,859
Continuous elections and frequent changes in office offer criminal groups endless opportunities

81
00:05:07,859 --> 00:05:12,180
to renegotiate agreements and infiltrate new candidates.

82
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:18,299
And we move from theory to the raw reality of facts documented by sensational cases.

83
00:05:18,299 --> 00:05:20,779
Let's take the municipality of Gricignano di Aversa.

84
00:05:20,779 --> 00:05:25,579
There, a mayor had made a real pre-election contract, promising future

85
00:05:25,579 --> 00:05:29,379
financial benefits to the Camorra clan in exchange for votes.

86
00:05:29,379 --> 00:05:34,740
This highlights the purely transactional aspect of this infiltrated politics.

87
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:39,899
Electoral support exchanged, in a completely calculated and explicit manner, for unlimited access

88
00:05:39,899 --> 00:05:41,979
to municipal coffers.

89
00:05:41,979 --> 00:05:43,379
Pure calculation.

90
00:05:43,379 --> 00:05:45,500
Construction and waste management.

91
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:50,860
And it is precisely towards these specific sectors of the legal economy that rivers of money are

92
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:51,860
funneled.

93
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:53,500
And it's no coincidence at all.

94
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:59,180
We are talking about sectors with very high capital intensity, where public contracts are generous

95
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:04,060
and where physical control of the territory, typical of the mafia, guarantees an almost unbeatable

96
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:07,060
illicit competitive advantage.

97
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:10,740
Statistical results confirm an unequivocal trend.

98
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:15,860
Infiltrated municipalities allocate, on average, many more resources to investments in construction

99
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:17,500
and waste management.

100
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:19,860
But here emerges an almost insane paradox.

101
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:24,300
Despite spending more on waste management, these same municipalities show

102
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:28,780
lower efficiency in collecting taxes for that same service.

103
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:33,540
In summary, on one hand, the taps of public spending are opened to feed businesses

104
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:38,540
close to the clans; on the other hand, a blind eye is turned to tax collection, creating a double,

105
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:41,500
very heavy damage to the community's coffers.

106
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:47,139
Obviously, to ensure that chosen candidates win and that this flow of money continues,

107
00:06:47,139 --> 00:06:51,539
criminal organizations leave absolutely nothing to chance.

108
00:06:51,539 --> 00:06:56,620
Investigations carried out in Seminara, Calabria, describe a methodical action of vote-buying.

109
00:06:56,620 --> 00:06:57,620
vote-buying.

110
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:02,219
Infiltration doesn't simply rely on the hope of winning; it secures control

111
00:07:02,219 --> 00:07:07,979
of the democratic process through illicit exchanges, directly influencing the will of the electorate

112
00:07:07,979 --> 00:07:09,899
to secure their own business interests.

113
00:07:09,899 --> 00:07:15,300
The true economic cost, all this mechanism inevitably leads us to reflect

114
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:19,660
on the long-term systemic consequences for the entire legal economy.

115
00:07:19,660 --> 00:07:25,220
The distorted allocation of funds hinders real development, stifles free competition

116
00:07:25,220 --> 00:07:30,340
among healthy businesses and, worse still, drains vital resources that should be invested

117
00:07:30,340 --> 00:07:34,500
in essential services like education or social infrastructure.

118
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:39,100
The result: entire territories are condemned to chronic underdevelopment.

119
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:43,860
The absolute gravity of the situation is summarized in this bitter contrast.

120
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:49,020
On one hand, the legitimate purpose of public funds intended for the well-being of the community.

121
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:55,420
On the other, those same resources systematically diverted towards the strategic sectors of crime.

122
00:07:55,420 --> 00:08:01,100
This strategy of silence, made of signatures, resolutions, and manipulated tenders, proves as dangerous,

123
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:04,500
if not more so, than explicit violence.

124
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:10,340
It corrodes institutions from within, slowly, without ever raising the outcry of a violent act.

125
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:14,660
Academic research thus reveals a sophisticated and harmful mechanism,

126
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:20,900
in which white-collar individuals and organized crime merge their interests to the detriment of us all.

127
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:26,100
And if we think that the dissolution of municipalities is the only visible tool to measure this phenomenon,

128
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:30,540
a truly unsettling final question emerges, one worth reflecting on.

129
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:34,100
What we have measured so far is only the tip of the problem.

130
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:36,660
How deep, truly, is the hidden layer,

131
00:08:36,659 --> 00:08:39,740
the iceberg of the infiltrated economy in Italian municipalities,

132
00:08:39,740 --> 00:08:44,219
constantly concealed beneath the silent surface of daily bureaucracy?